<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malmedy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malmedy is a town in the province of Liège in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. It grew around the abbey founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remaclus">Saint Remaclus</a> in the 7th century, twin of the abbey at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavelot">Stavelot</a>, with which it formed an ecclesiastical principality. Given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815, it was transferred to Belgium in 1920, and in December 1944 American prisoners were massacred near the town by German troops.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malmedy.jpg" alt="Malmedy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CathLegrand · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_de_Malmedy_en_mai_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Malmedy is a city and municipality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a> in the province of Liège. It lies on the Warche river, whose regular floods are said to have given the town its name, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> phrase A malo mundarum, meaning &quot;purifying from evil&quot;. The municipality has three districts: Malmedy, Bellevaux-Ligneuville and Bévercé, which includes the hamlets of Baugnez and Xhoffraix. A portion of the Spa-Francorchamps motor-racing circuit lies within it, although most of the circuit is in neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavelot">Stavelot</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remaclus">Saint Remacle</a>, abbot of Solignac Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, founded the town in 648 with a Benedictine monastery. From then until 1794 its history is that of the Princely Abbey of Stavelot-Malmedy, a clerical microstate ruled by a prince-abbot. The two towns formed one principality for 1,146 years under seventy-seven successive prince-abbots, but a rivalry grew between them, because Remacle chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavelot">Stavelot</a> as the main city. From the 16th century cloth, leather and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> industries appeared, and in the 17th century the principality became the most important tannery centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Papermaking was the main industry and brought the town considerable wealth.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the principality in 1795, and Malmedy became a lower prefecture in the département of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ourthe">Ourthe</a>. In 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> joined the Romance and Walloon town to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Its people kept the right to use French, even in the town council, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was founded after the Franco-German War of 1870. Under Chancellor Bismarck a policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> banned French lessons in schools and forbade priests to preach in French. Malmedy was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, after a plebiscite in which pro-German voters had to register their names; it was formally annexed in 1920 and joined the province of Liège in 1925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Between 1940 and 1945 Malmedy was again part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-bulge">Battle of the Bulge</a> in 1944 the area saw the Malmedy massacre, in which SS troops under Joachim Peiper killed 84 American prisoners of war. On 21 December 1944 German forces under Otto Skorzeny attacked the town, then held by American troops, and were repelled. On 23, 24 and 25 December United States aircraft bombed it repeatedly in friendly-fire incidents that killed about 200 civilians.</p><p>The town has about 12,000 inhabitants, roughly 95 per cent of them French speakers, with a German-speaking minority that has language facilities. Walloon, the historical language, is spoken mostly by older people but survives in the songs of the Cwarmê, the carnival held from Shrove Friday to Shrove Tuesday, in which the Haguète and the Long-Né are among the traditional costumes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malmedy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its territory, however, bore most of Germany's losses under the Treaty of Versailles: Eupen-<b>Malmedy</b>, the Hultschiner Ländchen, Danzig, the Memel Territory and much of Posen and West Prussia, leaving East Prussia cut off by the Polish Corridor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the First World War Belgium gained Eupen and <b>Malmedy</b> from Germany and the League of Nations mandate of Ruanda-Urundi, where its administration hardened the distinction between Hutu and Tutsi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 648 to 651 the missionary Remaclus founded twin abbeys at Stavelot and <b>Malmedy</b> in the Ardennes forest, on land granted by Sigebert III of Austrasia.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-stavelot-malmedy">Principality of Stavelot-Malmedy</a></li></ul></section>
